<!-- CHAPTER ONE -->
<h1>CHAPTER ONE: PROJECT PLANNING AND ANALYSIS</h1>

<h2>Statement of Problem</h2>
<p>Despite the growth of the digital economy, many small and medium-sized organizations continue to manage their internal network operations using semi-automated methods, such as paper-based logbooks, manual router command lines, and fragmented spreadsheets. These traditional methods present significant operational challenges, including frequent bandwidth inaccuracies, difficulty in tracking historical data usage, and a general lack of centralized security (Laudon & Laudon, 2022).</p>
<p>In the specific network facility under study, <strong>NetUsage Hub in Kisii</strong>, the absence of a centralized management system has led to critical inefficiencies. Currently, device inventory tracking and bandwidth usage recording are handled manually, leading to human errors and unmonitored traffic spikes. Furthermore, decision-making regarding bandwidth allocation and link restocking is based on administrative guesswork rather than empirical telemetry data. This fragmentation makes it nearly impossible for the administrator to analyze trends, such as identifying top bandwidth-consuming devices or calculating real-time daily traffic. As the volume of networked devices grows, these manual processes become unsustainable, causing operational bottlenecks, network congestion, and potential revenue loss. There is, therefore, a need to develop a centralized, secure internal management portal that automates device tracking, simplifies metric logging, and provides real-time network analytics. Such a system is essential for improving administrative efficiency, ensuring record accuracy, and enabling evidence-based business decisions.</p>

<h2>Study Justification</h2>
<p>The development of this project is justified by the critical need for digital transformation within the internal operations of network operations centers and enterprise internet hubs. While the telecommunications sector has modernized, many independent network facilities still struggle with manual record-keeping, leading to bandwidth inconsistencies and unreliable performance reporting (Rashid et al., 2025). This project addresses these gaps by providing an automated, centralized platform for internal management.</p>
<p>From a management perspective, this study is significant as it eliminates the reliance on paper-based ledger systems and unorganized spreadsheets. By implementing a secure internal portal, the network administrator can maintain data integrity through automated status updates and real-time transaction logging. This reduces human error and prevents operational bottlenecks caused by unaccounted traffic or device downtime. Furthermore, the project is justified by its contribution to informed decision-making. The integration of a traffic analytics dashboard allows the administrator to quickly identify top bandwidth-consuming devices and monitor daily usage trends. This shifts the business from a reactive "guesswork" approach to a proactive, data-driven strategy. Ultimately, this system improves overall operational efficiency and ensures that the network hub can scale its internal processes to meet increasing demand without increasing administrative workload.</p>

<h2>System Objectives</h2>

<h3>General Objective</h3>
<p>To develop a network usage monitoring system for NetUsage Hub.</p>

<h3>Specific Objectives</h3>
<p>1. To develop a device inventory management module for adding, editing and deleting device records, categories and bandwidth limits.</p>
<p>2. To develop an automated metric processing mechanism that captures network usage and logs transactions in real-time.</p>
<p>3. To implement a threshold alert system that automatically flags devices exceeding configured bandwidth limits.</p>
<p>4. To integrate a search functionality for quick retrieval of device details using names, IP addresses or categories.</p>

