<h2>Breakdown of Tools & Resources to Be Used</h2>
<h3>1. Software Tools</h3>
<p><strong>Full-Stack Framework:</strong> PHP – lightweight server-side scripting environment used to handle both front-end integration and back-end logic, including routing, API handling, and security.</p>
<p><strong>Front-End:</strong> HTML, CSS, JavaScript – used for structuring web pages, styling the interface, ensuring responsiveness, and adding interactivity.</p>
<p><strong>Database:</strong> MySQL – a relational database system used for storing application data in a secure, structured format.</p>

<h3>2. Development Environment</h3>
<p><strong>IDE:</strong> Visual Studio Code – for writing and managing front-end and back-end code.</p>
<p><strong>Version Control:</strong> Git and GitHub – Git for local version control to track changes in the source code, while GitHub was used as a remote repository for backup, collaboration, and project management.</p>

<h3>3. Deployment Resources</h3>
<p><strong>Application Hosting:</strong> XAMPP / Apache Web Server will be used to host the web application and manage the MySQL database in a live local and network environment.</p>
<p><strong>Application Structure:</strong> The front-end is integrated and served through PHP includes and public assets within the same application.</p>

<h3>4. Hardware Resources</h3>
<p><strong>Client Devices:</strong> Desktop computers, laptops and smartphones for accessing and testing the system.</p>
<p><strong>Server Machine:</strong> Local development PC running XAMPP.</p>
<p><strong>Networking Devices:</strong> Internet-enabled devices, routers and switches for system access and telemetry updates.</p>

<h3>5. Human Resources</h3>
<p><strong>System Developer:</strong> Responsible for system design, development, testing and documentation.</p>
<p><strong>Test Users:</strong> Classmates and network technicians.</p>
<p><strong>Project Supervisor:</strong> Provides guidance, monitoring, and evaluation throughout the project.</p>

