<h2>Logic Models</h2>

<h3>Log in process flowchart.</h3>
<div class="diagram-box">
(Start) --> [Enter Username and Password] --> &lt;Validate Credentials&gt;<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;|<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[Valid: Yes]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[Valid: No]<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[Successful] --------> (End)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;|<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[Error] -- (Return to Input)
</div>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.1 login process flowchart</div>
<p>This flowchart illustrates the steps involved when a user logs into the system. It shows how user credentials are entered, validated, authenticated and either granted access or denied based on correctness.</p>

<h3>System flow chart</h3>
<p>The flowchart illustrates how a network usage monitoring system operates from start to finish. The process begins with user login, where the system verifies the user’s credentials. If the login is invalid, an error message is displayed; if valid, the user is directed to the dashboard. From there, the user selects an operation such as adding, updating, deleting, or viewing items. Each option allows interaction with the database, where device details are entered, modified, removed, or displayed accordingly. After completing these actions, the system may generate a report based on the inventory data. Finally, the process ends once the user completes their tasks. This flow ensures efficient management, accurate record keeping, and easy access to network telemetry.</p>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.2 system flowchart</div>

<h3>Data flow diagrams</h3>
<div class="diagram-box">
[ Network Administrator ]<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| (Login, Device Data, Telemetry Readings)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v<br>
[ 0. Network Usage Monitoring System ]<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;|<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+---> (Stock & Bandwidth Alerts)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+---> (Reports & Telemetry Data)
</div>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.3 DFD</div>
<p>The Level 1 Data Flow Diagram illustrates how the network usage monitoring system is divided into four main processes: login, inventory management, metric processing, and report generation. The administrator interacts with the system by entering login details, managing device records, recording metrics, and viewing reports. Each process communicates with specific data stores, namely the User Database, Devices Database, and Usage Database, ensuring proper data storage, retrieval, and system functionality.</p>

<h3>Entity relationship diagram</h3>
<div class="diagram-box">
[ User ] --&lt; Manages &gt;-- [ Device ] --&lt; Records &gt;-- [ Metric / Alert ]<br>
Attributes: User(user_id, username, password)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Device(device_id, name, ip, limits, status, category)<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Metric(metric_id, device_id, download, upload, date)
</div>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.4 ER Diagram</div>
<p>The Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) represents the database structure of the network usage monitoring system. It consists of main entities: User, Device, Metric, and Alert. The User entity stores login credentials for system access. The Device entity contains details about each hardware node, including name, IP address, status, and bandwidth limit. The Metric entity records telemetry details such as download, upload, and recorded date. The relationships show that one user can manage many devices, while each device can be associated with multiple usage logs and alerts. The Metric entity includes a foreign key (device_id) to link each reading to a specific device, ensuring data integrity and proper tracking of transactions.</p>

<h3>Use case diagram</h3>
<p>The use case diagram for the Network Usage Monitoring System illustrates the interactions between the administrator and the system. The actor, represented by a stick figure, can perform several functions within the system boundary, including login, managing devices (adding, editing, deleting), viewing inventory, logging metrics, viewing transaction logs, generating reports, and searching devices. Each functionality is represented as an oval, and lines connecting the actor to the use cases show the interactions. This diagram helps visualize the system’s main features and the user’s role in performing tasks efficiently.</p>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.5 use case diagram</div>

<h3>Class diagrams</h3>
<p>The class diagram for the Network Usage Monitoring System shows the main classes in the system and how they relate to each other. The User class represents the administrator who can log in and manage operations. The Device class stores details about each hardware device, such as name, IP address, and bandwidth cap. The Metric class records transaction information, including the date and total bytes transferred, and is linked to both the user and the devices monitored. The Report class generates reports based on metric data. The relationships between these classes illustrate how users manage devices, metrics belong to devices, and reports are generated from metrics, providing a clear structure of how the system functions.</p>
<div class="caption">Figure 2.3.6 class diagram</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

