<h2>Logic Development</h2>

<h3>Login</h3>
<p>The login process uses a standard PHP PDO workflow. It first checks if a user is already authenticated; if so, it redirects them to the dashboard. For new login attempts, the system extracts the username and password from a POST request, searches the database for a matching user, and verifies the password hash. If the credentials are valid, it initializes a session and redirects the user to their intended destination. If authentication fails, it triggers an error message and reloads the login template.</p>

<pre>// authentication function (includes/auth.php)
function attempt_login(string $username, string $password): bool {
    $stmt = db()->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? LIMIT 1');
    $stmt->execute([$username]);
    $record = $stmt->fetch();
    if ($record && password_verify($password, $record['password_hash'])) {
        session_regenerate_id(true);
        $_SESSION['user'] = ['id' => $record['id'], 'username' => $record['username'], 'full_name' => $record['full_name'], 'role' => $record['role']];
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}</pre>
<div class="caption">Figure 3.3.1 PHP-based user authentication function implemented using PDO</div>

<h3>Dashboard</h3>
<p>The /logout route requires the user to be logged in, logs them out, shows a success message with session flash, and then redirects them to the login page. The dashboard route also requires login and displays summary statistics: it counts the total number of devices, counts active threshold alerts, calculates today’s total traffic using a database aggregate function (sum of download and upload filtered by today’s date), retrieves the most recently added devices, and passes all this data to the dashboard template for rendering.</p>

<pre>// dashboard metrics (public/dashboard.php)
$stats = [
    'devices' => (int)db()->query('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM devices')->fetchColumn(),
    'online' => (int)db()->query("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM devices WHERE status='online'")->fetchColumn(),
    'alerts' => (int)db()->query('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM alerts WHERE is_resolved=0')->fetchColumn(),
    'today_mb' => (float)db()->query('SELECT COALESCE(SUM(download_mb + upload_mb),0) FROM usage_metrics WHERE DATE(recorded_at)=CURDATE()')->fetchColumn()
];
$devices = db()->query('SELECT * FROM devices ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 8')->fetchAll();</pre>
<div class="caption">Figure 3.3.2 dashboard backend development</div>

<h3>Inventory</h3>
<p>The /inventory route displays all devices and highlights threshold items. The add route allows logged-in users to create a new device by submitting a form, saving it to the database, and redirecting to the inventory page with a success message. The edit route lets users update an existing device’s details, pre-filling the form and saving changes to the database. The delete route deletes a device (and its related records) from the database. The quick_log route updates traffic, records the metric, and triggers threshold alerts when limits are exceeded. Finally, the search route allows users to search devices by name, IP, or type using a query and displays the matching results.</p>

<pre>// inventory operations (public/devices.php)
if ($action === 'save') {
    $id = (int)($_POST['id'] ?? 0);
    $data = [trim($_POST['name']), trim($_POST['ip_address']), trim($_POST['device_type']), trim($_POST['location']), $_POST['status'], (float)$_POST['download_limit_mb'], (float)$_POST['upload_limit_mb']];
    if ($id) {
        $data[] = $id;
        db()->prepare('UPDATE devices SET name=?, ip_address=?, device_type=?, location=?, status=?, download_limit_mb=?, upload_limit_mb=? WHERE id=?')->execute($data);
    } else {
        db()->prepare('INSERT INTO devices (name, ip_address, device_type, location, status, download_limit_mb, upload_limit_mb, last_seen) VALUES (?,?,?,?,?,?,?,NOW())')->execute($data);
    }
} elseif ($action === 'delete') {
    db()->prepare('DELETE FROM devices WHERE id=?')->execute([(int)$_POST['id']]);
}</pre>
<div class="caption">Figure 3.3.3 inventory backend development</div>

<h3>Sales logs and reports</h3>
<p>This PHP code defines two authenticated pages. The transaction logs route retrieves all metric records from the database, ordered by recorded_at in descending order, and passes them to the template for display. The reports route generates summary data: it calculates the total bandwidth by summing usage_metrics, and finds the top best-selling / consuming devices by summing traffic per device, grouping by device id, ordering by total in descending order. These results are then rendered in the reports template.</p>

<pre>// metric and reporting backend (public/reports.php)
$stmtTotal = db()->prepare('SELECT COALESCE(SUM(download_mb + upload_mb),0) FROM usage_metrics WHERE recorded_at BETWEEN ? AND ?');
$stmtTotal->execute([$from.' 00:00:00', $to.' 23:59:59']);
$totalBandwidth = (float)$stmtTotal->fetchColumn();

$stmtTop = db()->prepare('SELECT d.name, SUM(m.download_mb + m.upload_mb) AS total_mb 
    FROM usage_metrics m JOIN devices d ON d.id=m.device_id 
    WHERE m.recorded_at BETWEEN ? AND ? GROUP BY d.id ORDER BY total_mb DESC LIMIT 5');
$stmtTop->execute([$from.' 00:00:00', $to.' 23:59:59']);
$topDevices = $stmtTop->fetchAll();</pre>
<div class="caption">Figure 3.3.4 sales backend development</div>

<h2>Testing</h2>
<p><strong>User Login Feature</strong><br>
Testing Action: I attempted to log in using both valid and invalid credentials to verify whether access was granted correctly and error messages were displayed.<br>
