Correction: I updated the login validation code to display an appropriate error message when credentials are invalid.</p>

<p><strong>Inventory Entry Form</strong><br>
Testing Action: I added sample products to the inventory to check if the details were correctly saved to the database.<br>
Result: Correctly saved.</p>

<p><strong>Sales Log Recording</strong><br>
Testing Action: I recorded sample metric transactions to verify that the system updated inventory quantities and calculated totals correctly.<br>
Result: The system initially did not evaluate threshold alerts after a metric record.<br>
Correction: I fixed the threshold evaluation logic so that alerts automatically trigger when limit is exceeded.</p>

<p><strong>Report Generation</strong><br>
Testing Action: I generated inventory and bandwidth reports for different dates to check the accuracy of totals and summaries.<br>
Result: Some entries were missing due to incorrect date filtering.<br>
Correction: I corrected the filtering logic, and all relevant data now appears in the reports.</p>

<p><strong>System Navigation and Buttons</strong><br>
Testing Action: I navigated through all menus and clicked buttons to verify that each directed to the correct page or performed the intended action.<br>
Result: Worked perfectly.</p>

<h2>Deployment</h2>
<p>The Network Usage Monitoring System has been successfully deployed on XAMPP and Apache web server, providing a reliable and accessible web-based platform for managing network inventory. Apache offers a local and intranet hosting environment, which ensures the application runs continuously without dependency on external public cloud servers. The deployment process involved placing the project in the htdocs root, configuring the MySQL database with required tables, and setting up the web app with the correct configuration parameters. This deployment enables users to access the system from any device with network connectivity, ensuring real-time inventory tracking, seamless data management, and efficient operational workflows, all hosted securely in the network.<br>
Access the live system here: <strong>Network Usage Monitoring System (NetUsage Hub)</strong></p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER FOUR -->
<h1>CHAPTER FOUR: CONCLUSION AND RECOMMENDATION</h1>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The aim of this project was to develop a Network Usage Monitoring System that improves the efficiency of managing network operations. Chapter One discussed the problem statement, justification of the study, and the objectives of the system. It highlighted the challenges faced by network hubs that rely on manual record keeping such as bandwidth inaccuracies and difficulty in tracking device performance. Chapter Two focused on the design and modelling of the system using diagrams such as the Data Flow Diagram, Entity Relationship Diagram, Use Case Diagram, and Class Diagram to illustrate how the system operates. Chapter Three described the implementation of the system including the development of the user interface, logic implementation using the PHP and MySQL framework, system testing, and deployment.</p>
<p>The developed system successfully automates important network operations including device inventory management, metric logging, and report generation. The system helps the network administrator track bandwidth levels, record transactions, and generate reports for better decision making. During the development of this project, I gained new knowledge in web development using PHP, database management using MySQL, and system deployment using local server platforms. However, some challenges were encountered such as debugging code errors and ensuring proper integration between the front-end and back-end components. These challenges helped improve my problem-solving and programming skills.</p>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<p>Although the Network Usage Monitoring System functions effectively, there are several improvements that future developers can implement to enhance its capabilities. One improvement would be integrating automated SNMP device polling so that network metrics can be gathered automatically instead of relying on manual entry.</p>
<p>Another improvement would be the integration of SMS and email notification technology. This would allow critical threshold alerts to be dispatched immediately to technicians' mobile phones, thereby improving response time and reducing network downtime.</p>
<p>Future developers may also consider upgrading the system database to a distributed cluster to support larger amounts of telemetry data and multiple concurrent users. Additionally, the system can be enhanced by implementing advanced analytics and graphical reports that provide deeper insights into traffic patterns and network performance.</p>

